The Southmark Petition was Rakuen's first formal annexation request to Midgaard during the early Ash-and-Iron decades.
Rakuen argued that southern hazard burdens had exceeded municipal capacity and required binding support obligations, not conditional recognition.
Midgaard denied the petition on ledger-compliance non-conformity grounds.
The refusal split Rakuen's political class into assimilation and compact-purist camps, beginning the long annexation era.
